        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Side sleepers need a bed that lets the shoulder and hip sink while the waist stays up. Here is how to get both, matched to your weight.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#helix-midnight-luxe"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Rankings
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="10.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#pressure-relief" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Shoulder and Hip Pressure</a>
          <a href="#firmness-by-weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Firmness by Body Weight</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#foam-vs-hybrid" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Foam vs Hybrid</a>
          <a href="#hip-and-back-pain" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Hip Pain and Back Pain</a>
          <a href="#combination-sleepers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Combination Sleepers</a>
          <a href="#buying-and-trial" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Trials, Sizes and Toppers</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best mattress for side sleepers does two things at once: it gives way under the shoulder and hip, and it holds the waist up so the spine stays level. Most side sleepers find that balance between 4 and 6 on the firmness scale, depending on their weight. These are the seven beds we would shortlist.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="pressure-relief" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Pressure Relief at the Shoulder and Hip: The First Thing to Get Right
          </h2>

          <p>
            When you lie on your side, most of your weight rests on two points: the shoulder and the hip. Those are also the widest parts of your body. On a mattress that does not give, the shoulder and hip take the load on a small area of skin, muscle and joint, and the spine bends upward between them. That is why side sleepers on firm beds wake up with numb arms, sore shoulders and aching hips, and why they spend the night turning to relieve the pressure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Shoulder</h3>

          <p>
            The shoulder needs to sink further than any other part of the body, because it sticks out furthest from the line of the spine. If it cannot sink, the neck tilts, the arm underneath goes numb and the shoulder joint carries pressure all night. A comfort layer of at least 2 to 3 inches of soft foam, a Euro top or a polymer grid is what lets it sink.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Hip</h3>

          <p>
            The hip is heavier than the shoulder, so it sinks more easily, and the risk runs both ways. On a firm bed the bony point of the hip takes the pressure. On a soft bed without good support, the hip sinks too far and the waist bends downward. Side sleepers with hip pain need cushioning and support together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Comfort Layer Thickness Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Pressure relief comes from the top few inches of a mattress, not the support core. A side sleeper's shoulder typically needs to sink a few inches before the spine levels out, so a comfort layer that is only an inch or two thick may let the shoulder reach the firmer support layer underneath. That is why Euro tops, pillow tops and thicker foam layers matter more for side sleepers than for anyone else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs Your Mattress Is Too Firm</h3>

          <p>
            Waking with a numb or tingling arm, sore shoulders, aching hips, or a habit of turning many times a night all point to too little pressure relief. If your mattress is otherwise flat and supportive, a soft topper may fix it; our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">best mattress topper</a> covers which kind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs Your Mattress Is Too Soft</h3>

          <p>
            Lower back stiffness that eases within an hour of getting up, a feeling of sinking into a hollow, or a dip where you sleep suggest the opposite: the hips are sinking too far, and the spine is curving down at the waist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="firmness-by-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Firmness for Side Sleepers by Body Weight
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most side sleepers do best between 4 and 6 on the 10-point firmness scale, where 1 is softest and 10 firmest. Where you land in that range depends mostly on how much you weigh, because weight decides how far you sink into any given mattress.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Under 130 Pounds</h3>

          <p>
            Lighter side sleepers do not compress a mattress much, so they need a soft surface, around 4 to 5, for the shoulder to sink at all. A medium-firm bed that feels fine to a heavier partner can feel like a board. Soft foam beds and plush hybrids suit this group best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">130 to 230 Pounds</h3>

          <p>
            Most side sleepers fall here, and a medium feel, about 5 to 6, is the usual sweet spot. The comfort layer should be thick enough for the shoulder to sink, and the support layer firm enough to hold the waist. Medium hybrids such as the Helix Midnight Luxe sit squarely in this range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over 230 Pounds</h3>

          <p>
            Heavier side sleepers compress a mattress further, so a medium bed feels softer to them. A medium-firm hybrid, around 6 to 7, with a thick coil unit usually works better, because it gives pressure relief at the surface without bottoming out. See our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/">best mattress for heavy people</a> for beds built for that load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Couples of Different Weights</h3>

          <p>
            If you and your partner differ a lot in weight or position, choose a medium hybrid and let the lighter partner add a topper to their side, or look at split-firmness options. For more on mattress makers, see our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-brands/">best mattress brands</a>.
          </p>

