        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for side sleepers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most side sleepers, the best mattress is a medium hybrid with a soft comfort layer and zoned support, such as the Helix Midnight Luxe. It lets the shoulder and hip sink while keeping the waist supported. The Purple Plus suits those with sharp shoulder pressure, and budget shoppers can start with the Zinus Green Tea memory foam mattress.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What firmness is best for side sleepers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most side sleepers do best between 4 and 6 on a 10-point firmness scale. Lighter people under about 130 pounds usually prefer 4 to 5, average-weight sleepers 5 to 6, and people over 230 pounds often need a medium-firm hybrid around 6 to 7 so they do not sink through the comfort layers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for side sleepers with hip pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many side sleepers with hip pain prefer a plush hybrid or a mattress with a thick Euro top, such as the Nolah Evolution 15 in Plush. The soft top lets the hip sink while coils keep the waist supported. A pillow between the knees helps too. Talk to a doctor or physical therapist about pain that persists.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for side sleepers with back pain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Side sleepers with back pain usually need zoned support, which makes the middle of the bed firmer than the shoulder area so the waist does not sag. The Helix Midnight Luxe is built that way. A medium feel, around 6 out of 10, is a good starting point for most average-weight side sleepers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is memory foam or hybrid better for side sleepers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both work. Memory foam contours closely and isolates motion, which suits side sleepers who lie still and sleep cool. Hybrids add coils for waist support, airflow and easier movement, which suits heavier side sleepers, combination sleepers and hot sleepers. If you are unsure which suits you, a medium hybrid is the safer first choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do my shoulders hurt when I sleep on my side?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Shoulder pain on waking often means the mattress is too firm for side sleeping, so the shoulder cannot sink and takes pressure all night. A pillow that is too low can add to it. A softer mattress or a 2 to 3 inch topper may help; persistent shoulder pain is worth checking with a doctor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a topper make a firm mattress good for side sleeping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. If your mattress is flat and supportive but too firm, a 2 to 3 inch memory foam or latex topper can add the shoulder and hip cushioning a side sleeper needs. A topper cannot fix a mattress that sags in the middle, which needs replacing instead, because the topper simply follows the dip.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

